---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../lib/site';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — Business Lab"
  description="Why Business Lab exists, who it is for, and how its content is written and reviewed."
  path="about"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container column prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
      <h1>Business is a life skill, not a specialism.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Financial and business knowledge is usually passed down, not taught. If your family does
        not have it, school rarely fills the gap. Business Lab is an attempt to fill it for free.
      </p>

      <h2>The gap</h2>
      <p>
        Most advice about starting a business or managing money quietly assumes a starting point:
        savings you can risk, a parent who has done it before, an adviser to call, or a network
        that opens doors. Plenty of people have none of those things, and the advice that does
        reach them is usually attached to something for sale.
      </p>
      <p>
        Plenty of young people now have a phone and a data connection long before they have anyone
        to ask about a payslip, a loan offer or a message that looks like it came from their bank.
        Each edition names the figures behind that gap for its own region, with sources.
      </p>

      <h2>What Business Lab does differently</h2>
      <p>
        Everything here is free, written in plain language, and assumes you are starting from
        zero. Lessons use real numbers instead of theory, say plainly when something is difficult,
        and never recommend a product, a company or an investment.
      </p>
      <p>
        Content is written per edition. Banking access, currency stability, and what counts as a
        normal income vary enormously between countries, so the India, Europe and United States
        editions each explain the same principle through the money system you actually live in.
        The calculators let you switch currency.
      </p>

      <h2>Who it is for</h2>
      <p>
        Roughly ages 15 to 21 — old enough to earn, start something, or make a financial decision
        that matters, and young enough that these habits have decades to compound.
      </p>

      <h2>How content is handled</h2>
      <p>
        Lessons cite their sources and carry a last-reviewed date. Anything about money is
        education, never personalised advice: Business Lab does not tell anyone what to buy, sell, or
        hold. Market examples use data that is at least a month old. Contributors are credited by
        first name and country only, and writers under 18 need a parent or teacher to sign off.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the name comes from</h2>
      <p>
        {site.name} grew out of {site.owner}'s school club of the same name, built on one idea:
        business is a life skill rather than a specialism. This site is his college passion
        project, and it takes the club's idea from one school to anyone with a phone. It is built in the open (the{' '}
        <a href={site.repo} rel="noopener noreferrer">source code</a> is public) and will stay free.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections and contact</h2>
      <p>
        If you spot a mistake, want to suggest a lesson, or need to reach the project for any
        reason, email <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a>. Privacy and
        data requests go to the same address; see <a href={`${base}/privacy`}>Privacy</a>.
      </p>

      <p class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn" href={`${base}/#editions`}>Choose an edition</a>
        <a class="btn-link" href={`${base}/privacy`}>How your data is handled</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
